<TopicPage slug="subjects/data-science/intermediate/exploratory-data-analysis">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Exploratory Data Analysis is <strong>looking before modelling</strong> — John
    Tukey's 1962 argument ("The Future of Data Analysis", expanded into his 1977 book)
    that analysis should begin with pictures and questions rather than assumptions and
    significance tests. Tukey's position was radical at the time and is now simply how
    good work is done: plot everything, notice everything, <em>then</em> decide what
    deserves formal treatment. The purpose of EDA is understanding — what shape is
    this data, what relationships exist, what is weird here? — and its deliverable is
    a set of hypotheses and findings, not a model. Every strong model choice
    downstream was usually visible in a good EDA first.
  </p>
  <p>
    The discipline has a characteristic rhythm: plot, notice, question, re-plot. It
    is the data-science cousin of the cleaning lesson's profiling — profiling asked
    "can this data be trusted?", EDA asks "what does this data say?" — and the two
    interleave constantly, because the fastest way to discover a data-quality bug is
    a plot that makes no sense.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four-pass EDA checklist" widget="equationStep" config={edaCfg} />

  <h2>Pass one: each variable's distribution</h2>
  <p>
    A histogram or density plot per variable, read for four properties.
    <strong>Shape</strong>: symmetric, skewed, bimodal? A bimodal distribution is a
    discovery hiding in plain sight — two populations in one column (two customer
    types, two sensors, two eras) that any single-number summary will average into
    fiction. <strong>Centre</strong>: mean and median — and their gap, which measures
    skew. <strong>Spread</strong>: standard deviation, range, and the percentiles
    that survive outliers. <strong>Tails</strong>: are extreme values rare and light
    (measurement-ish) or heavy (power-law-ish)? The choice ripples forward: heavy
    tails punish linear models and reward medians and log scales. A worked read:
    order values on a retail dataset come out strongly right-skewed with a median
    order of 24 and a mean of 89 — the histogram shows most orders small and a thin
    spike of enormous ones; reporting "average order 89" everywhere is now a
    documented choice, not an accident.
  </p>

  <h2>Summaries that lie: the permanent warnings</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Anscombe's quartet</strong> (1973) is the founding demonstration: four
    small datasets with identical means, variances, correlations and regression
    lines — and, when plotted, completely different structures (a clean line, a
    perfect curve, a line distorted by one point, a vertical column held hostage by
    one outlier). The lesson: summary statistics compress; plots reveal; always plot.
    The modern sequel, the <strong>Datasaurus Dozen</strong> (Matejka and Fitzmaurice,
    2017), builds twelve datasets that all look like a dinosaur (and other shapes)
    while sharing the same summary statistics to two decimal places. The second
    permanent warning is the mean versus median gap: incomes, house prices and
    response times are all tail-dominated, and "the average customer" may be a
    person who does not exist. The habit that resolves both: never let a number
    travel without its distribution.
  </p>

  <h2>Pass two: relationships between variables</h2>
  <p>
    Scatter plots are the workhorse, read for direction (positive, negative, none),
    <strong>shape</strong> (linear or curved — a U-shape has near-zero correlation
    and would sail through a naive check), <strong>strength</strong> (tightness),
    and <strong>outliers</strong> (which can manufacture or destroy a correlation on
    their own — remove the one point and a strong relationship can vanish; add one
    and it can appear). Correlation matrices give the overview but inherit linear
    blinders; the standard pipeline is matrix first, scatter for anything important.
    Two habits worth forming early: <strong>jitter or alpha-blend</strong> dense
    scatter (thousands of overlapping points hide as a solid blob otherwise), and
    treat correlation as a question, not an answer — the relationship's mechanism
    (causal? confounded? coincidence at this sample?) is exactly what EDA cannot
    decide and therefore must flag honestly.
  </p>

  <h2>Pass three: segments, and Simpson's paradox</h2>
  <p>
    Aggregates are averages of segments, and the segments can disagree with the
    total. The canonical case is <strong>Simpson's paradox</strong> in the University
    of California, Berkeley's 1973 graduate admissions data: overall, men were
    admitted at 44% and women at 35% — evidence of bias on its face. But department
    by department, women were admitted at roughly equal or higher rates than men in
    most departments. The reversal came from application patterns: women applied
    disproportionately to the departments (English, statistics) with low admission
    rates for everyone, men to the departments (engineering) with high rates. The
    aggregate number was true and misleading simultaneously — the average of several
    opposite stories. The EDA discipline: slice every important relationship by
    its natural segments (by region, by cohort, by device, by channel) and compare
    the slices to the total. When they disagree, you have found the analysis's
    real structure — and usually the finding that matters.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the aggregate is the "real" number and segments
  are complications. No — the choice of aggregation level is a modeling decision
  with consequences, and paradoxes are the proof. The correct response to
  "what is the overall number?" is often "for whom?".</p>

  <h2>Pass four: time</h2>
  <p>
    Any column with a timestamp earns a line plot, and time plots have their own
    reading list. <strong>Trend</strong> (the long slope), <strong>seasonality</strong>
    (the repeating pattern — daily, weekly, yearly), <strong>shifts</strong> (level
    changes at specific dates — usually interventions, sometimes bugs), and
    <strong>spikes</strong> (outliers to investigate, not smooth away). The classic
    mistakes are all visual: y-axes chosen to hide or inflate slopes; windows
    chosen to start at a peak or end at a trough; connecting points across a data
    gap as if nothing happened. The professional reflexes: plot the full history
    first, mark known events on the line (the launch, the price change, the
    migration — the charts lesson's annotation habit, born here), and compute
    rolling aggregates only after seeing the raw series.
  </p>

  <h2>The deliverable: notes, hypotheses and the loop</h2>
  <p>
    EDA's output is a document, not a chart pile. Its sections are predictable and
    that is the point: data-quality findings (fed back to the cleaning lesson's
    pipeline); distribution notes; relationships worth testing; segments that
    behave differently; open questions ranked by interest; and the hypotheses —
    stated precisely enough that a later confirmatory analysis could refute them.
    That last clause is the method's ethical spine: <strong>exploration finds
    patterns; confirmation tests them on data the exploration did not use</strong>.
    Hunt through twenty slices and something will look significant by chance alone
    (the statistics lessons' multiple-comparisons trap); the honest workflow keeps
    the exploratory notebook and the confirmatory test separate, so the finding
    survives meeting fresh data. And the loop closes: model residuals are data
    too — plot them, and whatever structure remains is a pattern the model missed,
    sending you back into EDA with sharper eyes.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked EDA hour: one dataset, the full pass</h2>
  <p>
    Watch the method run on a realistic brief: a rideshare company's trip table
    (date, pickup zone, drop-off zone, distance, fare, tip, driver ID, rider ID).
    <strong>Distributions first</strong>: distance is right-skewed with a hard edge at
    0 and a suspicious spike at exactly 4.0 km (hypothesis: a default value filling
    missing GPS — a data-quality find already); fare is roughly linear in distance
    with a heavy tail; tip is zero for 60% of trips — a bimodal flag, not an error.
    <strong>Relationships</strong>: fare versus distance is tight and linear below
    30 km, then bends — the pricing has a long-distance discount, and a naive linear
    model would silently blend two regimes. <strong>Segments</strong>: tip percentage
    by pickup zone splits the city into three clean regimes (airport flat-rate
    tipping, downtown percentage tipping, one zone at exactly zero — a partner
    contract?). <strong>Time</strong>: trips per day show a weekly wave, a growth
    trend, one catastrophic dip on a single date — the outage, found in ninety
    seconds of looking. The hour's deliverable: four data-quality findings, two
    pricing-structure hypotheses, one tipping segmentation worth modelling, and a
    date to investigate. No model was fitted; the modelling just became easy.
  </p>

  <h2>EDA and the model: residuals are data too</h2>
  <p>
    The loop deserves its own section because it is where EDA stops being a
    preliminary and becomes a partner. Fit a model — even a simple line — and its
    <strong>residuals</strong> (what the model failed to explain, point by point)
    are a fresh dataset with the model's blind spots highlighted. Plot them
    against each predictor and against time: structure in the residuals means a
    pattern the model missed (curvature that a straight line flattened, seasonality
    the trend ignored, a segment that behaves differently); a funnel shape means
    the model's errors grow with its predictions; a handful of giant residuals
    mean outliers the fit bent around. This is how the EDA → model → EDA cycle
    actually advances: each model is a hypothesis about structure, and its
    residuals are the data's answer to it. Practitioners who skip the residual
    loop ship models whose failures are invisible; practitioners who run it treat
    every model as one more plot asking to be looked at.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The expert layer. <strong>Tukey's actual toolkit</strong> predates software:
    stem-and-leaf plots (histogram and data table fused), boxplots (his invention —
    median, quartiles and outliers as one glyph, still the best comparison plot
    going), and the five-number summary. Learning them is learning to see what
    each plot type is <em>for</em>. <strong>The exploratory/confirmatory
    distinction</strong> is the field's deepest methodological idea (Tukey again,
    with Frederick Mosteller): the two activities use statistics differently —
    exploration is generation, generous with patterns; confirmation is
    prosecution, hostile to them — and the reproducibility troubles of modern
    science are largely the two roles blurring. <strong>Interactive EDA</strong>
    (brushing, linked plots, drill-down) is the modern turbo button — the human
    eye is a superb pattern detector given the right query loop. And
    <strong>EDA on big data</strong> inverts an assumption: at scale you sample,
    because a 2-million-row histogram is no more informative than a 20,000-row
    one — the plot's resolution, not the row count, sets the information ceiling.
    Tukey's rule survives every technology change: look first, and look again
    after every surprise.
  </p>
  <p>
    A last technical note: <strong>transformations are EDA moves</strong>, not
    cosmetic ones. Right-skewed variables (incomes, durations, counts) often reveal
    their structure on a log scale — multiplicative processes become additive,
    and a log-log scatter turning a curve into a line is the discovery of a power
    law, one of the most consequential pattern families in data. Percentages and
    bounded values behave better as logits or with care at the boundaries; counts
    with many zeros need zero-inflated thinking rather than a straight mean. The
    craft habit: when a plot looks like nothing, transform and look again — the
    relationship you are missing may be there on the other scale. Tukey's own
    re-expression principle said exactly this: the scale is part of the
    measurement, and choosing it is an analysis decision with the same weight
    as the statistic itself. Re-express first, plot second, summarise last — the
    order is the method, and it survives every tooling change the field will
    ever have.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Tukey, "The Future of Data Analysis" (1962) and Exploratory Data Analysis (1977); Anscombe, "Graphs in Statistical Analysis" (1973); Matejka &amp; Fitzmaurice, "Same Stats, Different Graphs" (2017); Bickel, Hammel &amp; O'Connell, Science (1975); Wickham &amp; Grolemund, R for Data Science.</p>
